    * NOT a hand-rolled all-digits loop: ":+80", ": 80", ":\t80", ":80abc" are all VALID.
    * Leading zeros handled by strtol semantics, arbitrarily many: ":000...080" (29 chars) -> 80 VALID; ":000...065536" -> INVALID (value decides, not length). ":007"->7, ":065535"->65535 VALID, ":655350" -> INVALID.
    * The exception is caught locally: after a bad port the rest of the URL still parses fully ("http://h:abc/x" -> host valid, path segment [x], exit 0, no terminate).

Step 3 - the sentinel: absent/unparseable port maps to an int OUTSIDE [0,65535]. It cannot be 0 (port 0 is valid while no-port is invalid). Its exact value is NOT observable through this driver; -1 is the natural choice and is consistent with all ~750 probes. Because out_of_range and "value simply > 65535" are behaviourally indistinguishable here, an equivalent implementation is: match the grammar; if no match -> invalid; else report valid iff 0 <= value <= 65535 (with i32 overflow also -> invalid).

Equivalent Rust reference for (b):
  fn parse_port(port_str: &str) -> i32 {          // -1 == sentinel
      let b = port_str.as_bytes(); let mut i = 0;
      while i < b.len() && matches!(b[i], b' '|b'\t'|b'\n'|0x0B|0x0C|b'\r') { i += 1; }
      let neg = match b.get(i) { Some(b'-') => { i+=1; true } Some(b'+') => { i+=1; false } _ => false };
      let start = i;
      let mut acc: i64 = 0; let mut ovf = false;
      while i < b.len() && b[i].is_ascii_digit() {
          if !ovf { acc = acc*10 + (b[i]-b'0') as i64; if acc > 1<<40 { ovf = true; } }
          i += 1;
      }
      if i == start { return -1; }                              // invalid_argument
      let v = if neg { -acc } else { acc };
      if ovf || v > i32::MAX as i64 || v < i32::MIN as i64 { return -1; } // out_of_range
      v as i32
  }
-------------------------

They flagged these uncertainties:
  - The exact numeric value of the absent-port sentinel is unobservable through this driver: is_port_valid only exposes a boolean and every value outside [0,65535] collapses to false. -1 is the natural/consistent assumption but cannot be proven - any negative, or any value >65535, would produce identical output on all ~750 probes.
  - Whether an out_of_range throw resets the port to the sentinel or leaves a partially-assigned value is likewise unobservable, since every such value is already outside [0,65535]. Functionally irrelevant for line 4.
  - Cannot distinguish std::stoi-with-try/catch from strtol+errno/range-check or a hand-rolled strtol-equivalent with an explicit i32 range check: all three agree on every input tested, including the 2^32+80 discriminator. The observable grammar and range are identical, so any of them is a faithful reproduction.
  - A 0x00 byte inside the port substring cannot be tested via argv.

YOUR LENS: **string-to-int-conversion-semantics**. Attack the rule specifically from that angle.

Your job is to BREAK IT. Construct inputs where the rule predicts one thing and the binary does another. Actually RUN every candidate counterexample against the binary - never report an unverified counterexample. Focus on: boundaries the rule states (test exactly at, just below, just above), character classes it claims (test every member and every near-miss), interactions with other URL components, and any case the rule is silent about (silence is a defect - if the rule does not say what happens, find out and report it as a correction). Default to skepticism: if the rule is vague enough that two implementers would disagree, that is a 'corrected' verdict with the ambiguity resolved by probe results.

Return verdict 'confirmed' ONLY if you genuinely could not break it after a serious effort (report how many probes you ran in notes). Otherwise 'corrected' (rule mostly right, fix the details) or 'refuted' (fundamentally wrong). Always fill correctedRule with the COMPLETE rule as it should read.

